          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence, no matter what the angles between the diagonals are, if the diagonals are equal and they bisect each other, then the angles of the quadrilateral formed are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "9 \\bar { 0 } ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " each, and the opposite sides are equal. Thus, the quadrilateral is a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.159,
            0.848,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now we know how the wooden strips have to be put together to form the vertices of a rectangle! They should be equal and connected at their midpoints."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.212,
            0.843,
            0.337
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.156,
                0.212,
                0.843,
                0.337
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.043,
            0.325,
            0.382,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.043,
                0.325,
                0.382,
                0.546
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.342,
            0.849,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This method is actually used in practice to make rectangles. Carpenters in Europe use this method to get a rectangular frame. It is also known that farmers in Mozambique, a country in Africa, use this method while constructing houses to get the base of the house in a rectangular shape."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.484,
            0.805,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Process of Finding Properties"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.515,
            0.847,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As we have been seeing from lower grades, properties of geometric objects such as parallel"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.552,
            0.848,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "lines, angles, and triangles can be deduced through geometric reasoning. We will continue to deduce properties of special types of quadrilaterals in this chapter."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.605,
            0.848,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Once you have deduced a property of a quadrilateral, it is good to verify it with a real-world quadrilateral, either the quadrilateral constructed on paper or simply a surface having the shape of the quadrilateral."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.66,
            0.849,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you are not able to figure out the property using deduction, you could experiment by taking real-world quadrilaterals and observing the property through measurement. Note that these observations give useful insights about the property, but with them, we can only form a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "conjecture",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", that is, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a statement about which we are highly confident, but not yet sure if it always holds true.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " For example, constructing a few rectangles and observing through measurement that their diagonals bisect each other does not necessarily mean that this will always be the case—can we be sure that the 1000th rectangle we construct will also have this property? The only way we can be sure of this property is by justifying or proving the statement, just as we did in Deduction 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.87,
            0.835,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note to the Teacher:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Gently encourage students to deduce or justify properties. Whenever students face challenges in doing it, encourage them to experiment and observe, and use their intuition to figure out the properties."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
